  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Dry and trending milder through Friday followed by a \nreturn to isolated to scattered showers through the weekend and into \nearly next week.\n\nThe closed low which has been situated off the NY/NJ coast the past \nfew days will begin making better headway northeast today as an open \nwave. By late tonight, this feature is expected to be centered just \nsouth of the Canadian Maritimes. In response, upper level ridging is \nexpected to take place over the Appalachians. What was has been \ngenerally a northerly flow pattern across our region will \ntransition to southwest tonight. The result will be a loss of \nthe low- to mid- level cloud shield which has been blanketing \nmuch of the eastern half of our region.\n\nIncreasing heights and the onset of warm air advection will yield \nprogressively milder temperatures across the region through \nThursday and Friday.\n\nAs we reach Friday, the center of the upper ridge is expected to be \nshunted southeast of the region with the approach of a cold front. \nThis front may bring isolated showers to the far western \nsections of the region as early as late friday afternoon. \nHowever, chances will increase Friday night into Saturday as \nthe front moves into, and perhaps stalls over our region.\n\nTiming and impact are still low on the confidence meter, but \nmoisture from what now is Hurricane Polo over southern Baja \nCalifornia, may have progressed across southern CONUS by early next \nweekend to interact with this front. As such, rainfall amounts, \ncoverage and duration across our region may be impacted. To add\nto the timing/impact/interaction question mark, a northern \nstream shortwave trough may bring a reinforcing cold front to \nour area by late in the weekend.",
